title touchstone — one attempt, and every boundary it crosses

// ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
//  THIS IS THE SECOND DIAGRAM, AND docs/07 §3 SAYS IT IS THE ONE THAT MATTERS:
//  "the ordering across the SDK, LangGraph, the checkpointer, the span exporter
//   and Phoenix is where a design turns out to be wrong. When only one diagram is
//   drawn, draw that one." ⛔ We drew the flowchart first — the kind that section
//  calls "easy and usually already right in your head". This is the correction.
//
//  ⛔ IT REPLACES A DRAWING RATHER THAN ADDING ONE. docs/04 §4a carried a Mermaid
//     sequence of the same run; it is gone, and §4a points here. Two sequence
//     diagrams of one run is the drift D-036 exists to stop, and the one that
//     survives is the one that shows the process and network boundaries — which
//     is the whole reason docs/07 §3 asks for a sequence at all.
//     ⚠ Every line of §4a's ARGUMENT stayed there. Only the picture moved.
//
//  🔴 ⛔ ERASER SILENTLY DROPS `note over` IN A SEQUENCE DIAGRAM. NEVER USE IT.
//     MEASURED 2026-08-17: a probe with `note over A,B: …` and `note over B: …`
//     was accepted with no error, round-tripped VERBATIM in the API's own `code`
//     field, and rendered ZERO pixels for both. Stored, echoed back, invisible.
//     ⚠ THAT IS EXACTLY THE HAZARD IN THIS PORT. The Mermaid original carried
//       FOUR notes and three of them were the load-bearing claims — invariant 1,
//       invariant 14, and "no arrow here, the verdict is never returned to the
//       scorer". A faithful-looking port would have kept the arrows, dropped the
//       reasons, and passed every check we have, because nothing we run reads the
//       render for meaning. So each one is re-expressed as a MESSAGE LABEL or a
//       BLOCK LABEL below, and they are marked ⛔ where they land.
//     📐 What IS supported, all probed on a live render before use: participant
//        attributes (icon, color), `>`, `-->` dashed, self-messages, `loop`, and
//        `alt`/`else`. `opt` renders too and is not used — nothing here is optional.
//
//  🔴 ⛔ AND A SECOND SILENT DROP, WORSE THAN THE FIRST: AN OPENING `[` IN A
//     MESSAGE LABEL DELETES THE WHOLE MESSAGE. Measured on a five-message probe:
//     a label containing `setting_sources=[]` rendered NOTHING, a label containing
//     a lone `[` rendered NOTHING, a label containing a lone `]` rendered FINE, and
//     the plain labels either side rendered fine — so the parser reads `[` as the
//     start of an attribute block and swallows the line. The DSL round-tripped
//     verbatim, two exports returned an identical content hash, and all four edges
//     were clear. ⛔ **A whole message vanished and every check in this repo passed
//     it.** It bit THIS file on its first render: `Models > Claude` — the process
//     boundary, which is the single reason a sequence diagram was drawn — was
//     missing, and the label is now written in prose to avoid the bracket.
//     🎯 Milestone 8 in scripts/check-diagram.py exists because of this, and it is
//        a static grep. Nothing that reads the render could have caught it, because
//        the render is a picture of the wrong thing and looks entirely correct.
//     ⚠ Same family as DEF-015, where a `]` inside a flowchart label truncated the
//       guard's own regex — but that was a reader and this is the RENDERER, and
//       truncation leaves evidence while deletion does not.
//
//  📐 WHAT THIS SHOWS THAT diagrams/touchstone.eraser CANNOT. The flowchart draws
//     what exists and what may reach what. It cannot draw ORDER, and three of this
//     project's sharpest claims are ordering claims:
//       · the telemetry wiring — four steps whose ORDER is the whole content
//         (docs/04 §3). Get it wrong and you get traces that look right and have
//         no LLM spans. A flowchart of the same four boxes says nothing at all.
//       · the checkpoint lands AFTER every node, not at the end of the run.
//       · the scorer runs LATER, in a different process, from a committed file.
//     🎯 The last one is why the two halves of this page never touch.
//
//  ⛔ THE LOAD-BEARING ABSENCE, AND IT IS THE SAME ONE AS IN THE FLOWCHART:
//     there is NO message between `Graph` and `Score`, in either direction. The
//     lifelines are ordered so that the ONLY path between them runs through
//     `Spans` — a file on disk. That is D-007, and it is what lets CI score a
//     committed JSONL with no credential and no model call (D-014). ⚠ An absence
//     cannot be asserted by a picture, so `Score`'s own label carries it in words
//     as well, and tests/unit/test_invariants.py carries it in code.
//
//  📐 LIFELINE ORDER IS CHOSEN, NOT ALPHABETICAL. Left to right is roughly "how
//     far from the developer's process": CLI and run.py are local, the graph,
//     tools/ and models.py are in-process, `claude` is a subprocess, the API is
//     the network, then the two disks, then the scorer that runs after everything
//     above has exited. ⛔ Adjacency was spent on the FREQUENT pairs —
//     models→claude, spans→score — so the crossing edges are the rare ones.
//     ⚠ `Tools` sits BETWEEN Graph and Models, which costs Graph→Models its
//       adjacency, and it buys two things: Graph→Tools is adjacent, and left to
//       right inside one specialist hop now reads as TIME — tool first, then the
//       model call, which is the order docs/04 §2's span tree lists them in.
//
//  🔴 ⛔ THE PORT DROPPED A SCORED SPAN TYPE, AND NOTHING HERE CAUGHT IT (DEF-021).
//     The first version of this file had no `Tools` lifeline at all. The Mermaid it
//     replaces had one, `touchstone.tool.*` is one of the four span families in
//     docs/04 §2, and two of the metrics in §2's table — tool-call count and
//     truncation rate — are computed from spans that this picture did not draw.
//     ⚠ Every guard passed. Milestones 1–5 check that what IS written resolves;
//       milestone 8 catches a message the RENDERER ate. Nothing checks for a
//       participant that was never typed. It was found by reading the drawing being
//       replaced, line by line, against the drawing replacing it — which is the only
//       method that works and is not automatable from here.
//     🎯 So the rule this file adds: **a replacement is audited against the thing it
//        replaces, not against the spec.** The spec agreed with both versions.
// ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

CLI [icon: terminal, color: blue, label: "cli.py — P0 shipped `doctor`, P1.9 adds the rest"]
Run [icon: play, color: blue, label: "loop/run.py — the k-attempt driver"]
Graph [icon: cpu, color: blue, label: "agent/graph.py — StateGraph + SqliteSaver"]
Tools [icon: tool, color: blue, label: "tools/read.py + tools/runbooks.py — LOCAL reads, no network\nP2 also serves the same five over MCP (tools/mcp_server.py)"]
Models [icon: code, color: blue, label: "agent/models.py — a FILE, ~60 lines (DEF-002)"]
Claude [icon: cloud, color: grey, label: "`claude` CLI subprocess → the Anthropic API\nthe process boundary, then the network one"]
Tel [icon: activity, color: blue, label: "telemetry.py — P1.5, moved here by D-037"]
Ckpt [icon: database, color: grey, label: ".touchstone/checkpoints.db\n⚠ written every node, read back by NOTHING yet"]
Spans [icon: file-text, color: red, label: "results/spans/(version).jsonl\nthe ONLY thing that crosses from the run to the score"]
Score [icon: bar-chart-2, color: blue, label: "loop/score.py — reads SPANS, never a return value (D-007)\n⛔ it has no message to or from the graph, on purpose"]
Cmp [icon: filter, color: orange, label: "loop/compare.py — P2.4, the gate"]

// ── the wiring, and ORDER IS THE ENTIRE CONTENT (docs/04 §3) ──────────────────
// ⛔ These three are numbered in their labels because the numbers ARE the claim.
//    A flowchart would draw the same three boxes and assert nothing. Wrong order
//    produces a trace that looks correct and is missing every LLM span — which is
//    the shape of every quiet failure in this project.
CLI > Tel: init_telemetry() — ONCE, from cli.py
Tel > Tel: 1 · phoenix.otel.register() — BEFORE importing anything that instruments
Tel > Tel: 2 · LangChainInstrumentor().instrument() — nodes become spans
Tel > Tel: 3 · AnthropicInstrumentor().instrument() — model calls become LLM spans

CLI > Run: touchstone run vN --k 3
// ⛔ MERMAID NOTE 1 OF 4 LANDS HERE — "GroundTruth stays in truth.json, invariant 1".
//    It is on the message rather than beside it, because a note would not render.
Run > Run: seed from suite/benchmark/manifest.json — the Incident ONLY, truth.json stays shut (invariant 1)

loop [label: "k attempts — config.K = 3 (D-030) · a cached attempt is never re-run (D-015)"] {
  Run > Graph: invoke(state, thread_id=run_id)
  Graph > Tel: touchstone.run {version, case_id, attempt, tier, benchmark_hash}

  // ⛔ MERMAID NOTE 2 OF 4 IS THIS BLOCK'S LABEL — "no two specialist spans overlap".
  //    Better here than as a note: invariant 14 is a property OF THE LOOP, and the
  //    loop is the thing that would violate it by fanning out.
  loop [label: "one specialist per hop — no two node spans OVERLAP (invariant 14, D-026) · until next == done or hops ≥ MAX_HOPS (D-039)"] {
    // ⛔ TOOL BEFORE MODEL, and that ordering is read off docs/04 §2's span tree,
    //    where touchstone.tool.get_metrics is listed ABOVE the LLM span under the
    //    same node. It is a SPECIALIST-only hop — the tree shows no tool span under
    //    supervisor or synthesizer. ⚠ Nothing is built yet, so §2 is the authority
    //    here; if nodes.py ends up calling the model first, this arrow moves.
    Graph > Tools: a specialist hop only — supervisor and synthesizer call none (docs/04 §2)
    Tools --> Graph: the incident's own state, or BM25 over runbooks/ — truncated at the cap
    Tools > Tel: touchstone.tool.* {name, result_size, truncated} — a CHILD of the node span · tool count and truncation rate are scored off it
    Graph > Models: the node's model call — every node, no exceptions
    Models > Claude: subprocess · setting_sources is the EMPTY list, never None · the model is PINNED (D-034)
    Claude --> Models: ResultMessage — model_usage is where the model id was measured (D-033)
    Models --> Graph: the node's output
    Graph > Ckpt: checkpoint AFTER every node — not at the end of the run
    Graph > Tel: touchstone.node.* {hop, next, findings_seen} — findings_seen is for the human (D-025, D-043)
  }

  // ⛔ MERMAID NOTE 3 OF 4 IS THE TAIL OF THIS LABEL — "the run ends here, nothing
  //    waits, no gate span (D-040)". The verdict span IS the terminal event.
  Graph > Tel: touchstone.verdict {root_cause_id, blast_radius, escalate} — the run ENDS here · nothing waits, no gate span (D-040)
  Tel > Spans: the file exporter writes JSONL — timestamps included, or invariant 14 is unrunnable
}

// ── everything below happens LATER, in a different process ────────────────────
// ⛔ MERMAID NOTE 4 OF 4 — "no arrow here, the verdict is never returned to the
//    scorer" — has no message to ride on, because the whole claim is that there is
//    no message. It is carried by `Score`'s participant label, by the lifeline
//    order (the only path from Graph to Score runs through a file), and by
//    tests/unit/test_invariants.py in code. ⚠ THIS IS THE ONE THE PORT COULD HAVE
//    LOST SILENTLY, and it is the most important claim on the page.
Score > Spans: read the committed JSONL — NO model call, NO credential (D-014)
Spans --> Score: spans, with their timestamps
Score > Score: read truth.json — at SCORE time only, and by nothing else in this picture
Score > Cmp: results/(version).json — the scored half only · diagnostics{} reaches nothing (D-045)
Cmp > Cmp: five conditions against the incumbent — an AND, not five options

alt [label: "all five hold"] {
  Cmp --> CLI: PROMOTE — vN is the champion
}
else [label: "any ONE fails"] {
  Cmp --> CLI: REFUSE — and WHICH case blocked it. The refusal IS the result
}
